.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions are thin wrappers over the operating system interface.
Every function is implemented by one of three backends, chosen at build time:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Linux, freestanding
Available on x86_64 and aarch64.
Each wrapper issues the system call itself with inline
.Ql syscall
or
.Ql svc #0 .
On aarch64, which lacks the legacy calls,
.Fn xft_open ,
.Fn xft_stat
and
.Fn xft_mkdir
use the
.Ql *at
variants with
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
and
.Fn xft_fork
uses
.Xr clone 2 .
.It Linux, Dv XFT_LIBC
Each wrapper calls
.Xr syscall 2
with the same arguments; the C library wrappers such as
.Xr open 2
are not used.
This backend uses the
.Ql *at
variants and
.Xr clone 2
for
.Fn xft_fork
on every architecture.
It is the only backend for architectures other than x86_64 and aarch64.
.It Windows
Each wrapper is emulated with
.Pa kernel32
and
.Pa ntdll
calls, in both freestanding and
.Dv XFT_LIBC
builds.
A file descriptor is a
.Vt HANDLE
truncated to
.Vt int .
.El
.Pp
The header includes
.In types/timing_types.h ,
.In types/signal_types.h
and
.In types/io_types.h ,
which define
.Vt t_timespec
.Pq Xr xft_timing 3 ,
.Vt t_sigset
.Pq Xr xft_signals 3
and
.Vt t_iovec
.Pq Xr xft_io 3 .
.Ss Memory
.Fn xft_mmap
maps
.Fa size
bytes of private anonymous memory with protection
.Fa prot ,
a mask of
.Dv PROT_*
bits.
.Dv MAP_PRIVATE
and
.Dv MAP_ANONYMOUS
are always set and
.Fa flags_extra
is OR-ed in.
No address hint is given.
On Windows the range is only reserved with
.Ql VirtualAlloc(MEM_RESERVE)
and
.Fa flags_extra
is ignored.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mmap_commit
makes a range returned by
.Fn xft_mmap
usable.
On Windows it commits
.Fa size
bytes at
.Fa ptr
with
.Ql VirtualAlloc(MEM_COMMIT) .
On Linux it does nothing and returns
.Dv OK .
Portable code calls it after every
.Fn xft_mmap .
.Pp
.Fn xft_fmap
maps the first
.Fa size
bytes of the file open on
.Fa fd
read-only and private, at offset 0.
On Windows it creates a
.Dv PAGE_READONLY
file mapping, maps a view of it and closes the mapping handle.
.Pp
.Fn xft_map_failed
tests a pointer returned by
.Fn xft_mmap ,
.Fn xft_fmap
or
.Fn xft_mremap .
It is true when
.Fa ptr
lies in the last
.Dv XFT_MAX_ERRNO
(4095) bytes of the address space, which covers both
.Dv MAP_FAILED
and the negative error codes returned by the freestanding backend.
.Pp
.Fn xft_munmap
unmaps
.Fa size
bytes at
.Fa mem .
Errors are ignored.
On Windows
.Fa size
is ignored;
.Fa mem
is released with
.Ql VirtualFree(MEM_RELEASE) ,
or with
.Ql UnmapViewOfFile
if that fails, so it must be the base address of an
.Fn xft_mmap
or
.Fn xft_fmap
region.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mremap
resizes the mapping at
.Fa addr
from
.Fa size
to
.Fa new_size
bytes.
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE
is always set and
.Fa flags_extra
is OR-ed in, so the returned address may differ from
.Fa addr .
On Windows a new read-write region is mapped and committed, the first
.Ql min(size, new_size)
bytes are copied and the old region is released.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mprotect
sets the protection of
.Fa size
bytes at
.Fa addr
to
.Fa prot .
.Pp
On Windows
.Dv PROT_*
masks become page protections as follows: no access bits give
.Dv PAGE_NOACCESS ,
.Dv PROT_READ
gives
.Dv PAGE_READONLY ,
any mask with
.Dv PROT_WRITE
gives
.Dv PAGE_READWRITE ,
and
.Dv PROT_EXEC
selects the matching
.Dv PAGE_EXECUTE_*
value.
.Ss Files
.Fn xft_open
opens
.Fa path
with
.Fa flags ,
a combination of the
.Dv O_*
constants.
With
.Dv O_CREAT ,
a new file is created with permissions
.Fa mode ,
masked by the process umask; otherwise
.Fa mode
is ignored.
On Windows the file is opened with
.Ql CreateFileA ,
sharing read, write and delete access, with
.Dv FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS
so directories can be opened.
.Dv O_CREAT ,
.Dv O_TRUNC
and
.Dv O_EXCL
select the creation disposition, and
.Dv O_APPEND
requests write access without
.Dv FILE_WRITE_DATA .
.Pp
.Fn xft_close
closes
.Fa fd .
.Pp
.Fn xft_read
reads up to
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa fd
into
.Fa buffer .
.Fn xft_write
writes up to
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa buffer
to
.Fa fd .
On Windows
.Fa len
is clamped to
.Dv UINT32_MAX ,
and a read failing with
.Dv ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE
returns 0 as end of file.
.Pp
.Fn xft_writev
writes the
.Fa len
buffers described by the
.Vt t_iovec
array
.Fa buffers ,
whose
.Fa mem
and
.Fa size
members match the layout of
.Vt struct iovec .
On Windows it calls
.Fn xft_write
once per non-empty buffer and stops at the first short write; it is not atomic.
.Pp
.Fn xft_stat
fills
.Fa statbuf
with the status of
.Fa path ,
following symbolic links.
With
.Dv XFT_LIBC
on Linux,
.Vt t_stat
is
.Vt struct stat .
Otherwise it is the kernel layout for the architecture, with the members
.Fa st_dev ,
.Fa st_ino ,
.Fa st_nlink ,
.Fa st_mode ,
.Fa st_uid ,
.Fa st_gid ,
.Fa st_rdev ,
.Fa st_size ,
.Fa st_blksize ,
.Fa st_blocks ,
.Fa st_atim ,
.Fa st_mtim
and
.Fa st_ctim .
On Windows the members are synthesized: permission bits are 0444 for
read-only files and 0666 otherwise, directories get
.Dv S_IFDIR
and 0111, pipes get
.Dv S_IFIFO ,
other non-disk files get
.Dv S_IFCHR ,
.Fa st_blksize
is 4096, and
.Fa st_uid ,
.Fa st_gid
and
.Fa st_rdev
are 0.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mkdir
creates the directory
.Fa path
with permission bits
.Fa mode ;
.Dv XFT_MKDIR_0755
is provided for the common case.
On Windows
.Fa mode
is ignored.
.Pp
.Fn xft_fcntl
issues
.Xr fcntl 2
with
.Fa cmd
and the lock description
.Fa arg .
It is not available on Windows.
.Pp
.Fn xft_lockf
places a blocking lock over the whole file open on
.Fa fd .
On Linux it is a shared
.Dv F_RDLCK
lock taken with
.Dv F_SETLKW ;
on Windows it is an exclusive
.Ql LockFileEx
lock.
.Fn xft_unlockf
releases it with
.Dv F_UNLCK
and
.Dv F_SETLK ,
or
.Ql UnlockFileEx .
These are record locks, not
.Xr lockf 3 .
.Ss Processes and threads
.Fn xft_exit
terminates the caller with
.Fa status .
On Linux it issues
.Xr _exit 2 ,
not
.Xr exit_group 2 ,
in both backends: in a multithreaded process only the calling thread ends,
and with
.Dv XFT_LIBC
no
.Xr atexit 3
handlers run and no stdio buffers are flushed.
On Windows it calls
.Ql ExitProcess .
.Pp
.Fn xft_getpid
returns the process ID.
.Pp
.Fn xft_fork
creates a child process.
The freestanding x86_64 backend issues
.Xr fork 2 ;
the other Linux backends issue
.Xr clone 2
with only
.Dv XFT_SIGCHLD
as flags.
With
.Dv XFT_LIBC
the C library is bypassed, so its fork handlers do not run.
On Windows it fails.
.Pp
.Fn xft_clone
issues
.Xr clone 2
with the members of
.Fa args :
.Fa flags
.Pq clone flags OR-ed with the exit signal ,
.Fa stack
.Pq the child's stack pointer ,
.Fa ptid ,
.Fa ctid
and
.Fa tls .
The argument order of each architecture is handled internally.
The child resumes at the return of the system call on
.Fa stack ;
no entry function is called for it.
The
.Dv CLONE_*
constants are not exported by the public headers.
On Windows it fails.
.Pp
.Fn xft_wait4
waits for the child
.Fa pid
and stores its wait status in
.Fa status
if not
.Dv nullptr .
.Fa options
is a mask of
.Dv XFT_WNOHANG
and
.Dv XFT_WUNTRACED .
.Fa rusage
is passed through to the kernel and may be
.Dv nullptr .
On Windows
.Fa pid
must name one process,
.Fa rusage
and
.Dv XFT_WUNTRACED
are ignored, and the status is the low 8 bits of the exit code shifted left by 8.
.Pp
.Fn xft_set_tid_address
sets the address the kernel clears and wakes when the calling thread exits,
and returns the caller's thread ID.
On Windows
.Fa address
is ignored.
.Pp
.Fn xft_sigprocmask
changes the signal mask of the calling thread.
.Fa flags
is
.Dv XFT_BLOCK ,
.Dv XFT_UNBLOCK
or
.Dv XFT_SETMASK .
If
.Fa set
is not
.Dv nullptr
it is applied; if
.Fa oldest
is not
.Dv nullptr
it receives the previous mask.
The kernel is told the mask is
.Dv XFT_SIGSET_SIZE
bytes.
On Windows it does nothing except zero
.Fa *oldest .
.Ss Synchronization
.Fn xft_futex_wait
sleeps until woken by
.Fn xft_futex_wake ,
provided
.Fa *uaddr
still equals
.Fa val .
There is no timeout.
.Fn xft_futex_wake
wakes up to
.Fa val
threads sleeping on
.Fa uaddr .
On Linux these issue
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT
and
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE
without
.Dv FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG .
On Windows they use
.Ql RtlWaitOnAddress
with a 4-byte comparison, and
.Ql RtlWakeAddressSingle
when
.Fa val
is 1 or
.Ql RtlWakeAddressAll
when it is greater.
.Ss Time and scheduling
.Fn xft_clock_gettime
stores the current
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
time in
.Fa ts .
The clock cannot be chosen.
On Windows the time is derived from
.Ql QueryPerformanceCounter .
.Pp
.Fn xft_sched_getaffinity
stores the CPU affinity mask of
.Fa pid
in the
.Fa cpusetsize
bytes at
.Fa mask .
.Fn xft_sched_setaffinity
sets it.
On Linux a
.Fa pid
of 0 means the caller.
On Windows
.Fa pid
is a Windows process ID, only the 64 CPUs of the current processor group are
seen, the rest of
.Fa mask
is zeroed, and
.Fa cpusetsize
must be at least 8.
.Pp
.Fn xft_get_cpu_count
stores in
.Fa *count
the number of CPUs the process may run on: the population count of a
128-byte affinity mask, so at most 1024.
.Ss Linux only
These are declared in
.In linux.h ,
which is not included by
.In xft.h
and fails to compile on other systems.
.Pp
.Fn xft_ioctl
issues
.Xr ioctl 2
on
.Fa fd .
.Pp
.Fn xft_perf_event_open
opens a performance counter described by
.Fa attr
for the calling thread on any CPU, in the group led by
.Fa group_fd
.Pq \-1 for a new group ,
with
.Dv PERF_FLAG_FD_CLOEXEC .
With
.Dv XFT_LIBC ,
.Vt t_perf_event_attr
is
.Vt struct perf_event_attr .
Otherwise it is a copy of the kernel layout in which the kernel's anonymous
unions are the named members
.Fa period ,
.Fa wakeup ,
.Fa bp_addr ,
.Fa bp_len
and
.Fa aux ,
of types
.Vt t_linux_perf_period ,
.Vt t_linux_perf_wakeup ,
.Vt t_linux_perf_bp_addr ,
.Vt t_linux_perf_bp_len
and
.Vt t_linux_perf_aux .
The header then also defines
.Dv PERF_TYPE_HARDWARE ,
.Dv PERF_TYPE_SOFTWARE ,
the
.Dv PERF_COUNT_HW_*
and
.Dv PERF_COUNT_SW_*
event IDs it uses,
.Dv PERF_FORMAT_TOTAL_TIME_ENABLED ,
.Dv PERF_FORMAT_TOTAL_TIME_RUNNING ,
.Dv PERF_FORMAT_GROUP ,
.Dv PERF_FLAG_FD_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv PERF_IOC_FLAG_GROUP
and the
.Dv PERF_EVENT_IOC_ENABLE ,
.Dv PERF_EVENT_IOC_DISABLE
and
.Dv PERF_EVENT_IOC_RESET
requests for
.Fn xft_ioctl .
.Ss Constants
With
.Dv XFT_LIBC
on Linux the standard constants come from
.In sys/mman.h ,
.In fcntl.h
and
.In unistd.h .
Otherwise
.In syscalls.h
defines each one that is not already defined, with its Linux value:
.Dv PROT_NONE ,
.Dv PROT_READ ,
.Dv PROT_WRITE ,
.Dv PROT_EXEC ,
.Dv MAP_PRIVATE ,
.Dv MAP_ANONYMOUS ,
.Dv MAP_FAILED
(the integer \-1),
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE ,
.Dv F_RDLCK ,
.Dv F_WRLCK ,
.Dv F_UNLCK ,
.Dv F_SETLK ,
.Dv F_SETLKW ,
.Dv SEEK_SET ,
.Dv O_RDONLY ,
.Dv O_WRONLY ,
.Dv O_RDWR ,
.Dv O_ACCMODE ,
.Dv O_CREAT ,
.Dv O_EXCL ,
.Dv O_TRUNC ,
.Dv O_APPEND ,
.Dv STDIN_FILENO ,
.Dv STDOUT_FILENO
and
.Dv STDERR_FILENO .
On Windows the
.Dv STD*_FILENO
values are not handles; use the descriptors from
.Xr xft_io 3
instead.
.Pp
Always defined are
.Dv XFT_MAX_ERRNO
(4095),
.Dv XFT_WNOHANG
(1),
.Dv XFT_WUNTRACED
(2) and
.Dv XFT_MKDIR_0755
(0755).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions returning
.Vt int ,
.Vt t_i32 ,
.Vt t_i32a ,
.Vt t_i64a
or
.Vt t_ssize
return the non-negative result of the call on success and \-1 on failure.
The error code is not returned: the freestanding backend discards it, the
.Dv XFT_LIBC
backend leaves it in
.Va errno ,
and the Windows backend may leave it in
.Ql GetLastError .
.Pp
The results on success are those of the underlying call:
.Fn xft_open
returns a descriptor,
.Fn xft_read ,
.Fn xft_write
and
.Fn xft_writev
a byte count,
.Fn xft_getpid
the process ID,
.Fn xft_fork
and
.Fn xft_clone
0 in the child and the child's ID in the parent,
.Fn xft_wait4
the ID of the child waited for, or 0 if
.Dv XFT_WNOHANG
was given and it has not changed state,
.Fn xft_set_tid_address
the thread ID,
.Fn xft_futex_wake
the number of threads woken
.Pq always 0 on Windows ,
.Fn xft_sched_getaffinity
the number of mask bytes written, and the others 0.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mmap ,
.Fn xft_fmap
and
.Fn xft_mremap
return the address of the mapping or an error value, which must be tested with
.Fn xft_map_failed
rather than compared to
.Dv MAP_FAILED :
the freestanding backend returns the kernel's negative error code cast to a
pointer.
.Pp
.Fn xft_map_failed
returns non-zero
.Pq numerically Dv OK
if
.Fa ptr
is an error value and 0 otherwise; the result is a truth value, not a status.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mmap_commit
and
.Fn xft_get_cpu_count
return
.Dv OK
on success and
.Dv KO
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn xft_munmap
returns nothing.
.Fn xft_exit
does not return.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Map a file read-only, as
.Xr xft_io 3
does:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_buffer	map_file(const char *name)
{
	t_stat	st;
	int		fd;
	t_any	ptr;

	if (xft_stat(name, &st) != 0)
		return ((t_buffer){0});
	fd = xft_open(name, O_RDONLY, 0);
	if (fd == -1)
		return ((t_buffer){0});
	ptr = xft_fmap(st.st_size, fd);
	xft_close(fd);
	if (xft_map_failed(ptr))
		return ((t_buffer){0});
	return ((t_buffer){.size = st.st_size, .mem = ptr});
}
.Ed
.Pp
Allocate committed pages portably, as the page allocator does:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_any	get_pages(t_size size)
{
	t_any	mem;

	mem = xft_mmap(size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, 0);
	if (xft_map_failed(mem))
		return (nullptr);
	if (!xft_mmap_commit(mem, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, 0))
	{
		xft_munmap(mem, size);
		return (nullptr);
	}
	return (mem);
}
.Ed
